1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,186
Look at your first two evenings from the crew's side of the table.

2
00:00:04,086 --> 00:00:05,606
One of them belongs to Science.

3
00:00:06,056 --> 00:00:08,018
The other belongs to Weapons.

4
00:00:09,818 --> 00:00:11,907
If the next three are fights as well,

5
00:00:12,357 --> 00:00:14,673
half your crew spends a month watching.

6
00:00:16,473 --> 00:00:18,671
Today you'll plan who carries each evening,

7
00:00:19,121 --> 00:00:21,047
and you'll build two new kinds.

8
00:00:22,247 --> 00:00:24,514
A style is the answer to one question.

9
00:00:24,964 --> 00:00:26,053
Whose evening is it?

10
00:00:27,853 --> 00:00:30,129
A search is Science's evening.

11
00:00:31,029 --> 00:00:34,014
A fight belongs to Weapons and Engineering.

12
00:00:35,814 --> 00:00:37,330
A talk belongs to Comms.

13
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:39,079
A race belongs to Helm.

14
00:00:39,979 --> 00:00:43,391
Politics is Comms again, with the captain.

15
00:00:43,841 --> 00:00:46,452
And trade is Helm and Comms together.

16
00:00:48,252 --> 00:00:50,380
You can build all six of those today,

17
00:00:50,830 --> 00:00:52,117
with what you already know.

18
00:00:53,317 --> 00:00:55,346
There are four more styles in this course.

19
00:00:56,246 --> 00:00:58,660
Boarding, a ruin, a set battle,

20
00:00:59,110 --> 00:01:00,950
and the Admiral's view from above.

21
00:01:02,750 --> 00:01:05,803
A campaign in your universe can't use those yet.

22
00:01:06,703 --> 00:01:09,932
Each one's waiting on a lecture in the last class.

23
00:01:11,732 --> 00:01:15,666
So plan for them, and write them in your rotation as later.

24
00:01:16,566 --> 00:01:18,552
But don't build them today.

25
00:01:19,752 --> 00:01:22,674
Three rules, and they're habits, not laws.

26
00:01:24,474 --> 00:01:26,638
Never the same style twice running.

27
00:01:27,538 --> 00:01:30,339
Everyone carries an evening in every act.

28
00:01:31,239 --> 00:01:33,120
And a tentpole mixes two.

29
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:36,245
Now look at the last column,

30
00:01:36,695 --> 00:01:38,711
what's on offer beside the spine.

31
00:01:39,611 --> 00:01:41,861
Half an evening is the universe, remember.

32
00:01:42,311 --> 00:01:43,969
This is where you plan that half.

33
00:01:45,169 --> 00:01:47,948
Evening three is a stub, so let's make it an evening.

34
00:01:49,748 --> 00:01:51,700
The open now leads to an objective,

35
00:01:52,150 --> 00:01:54,092
and the objective ends with a signal.

36
00:01:54,992 --> 00:01:56,965
An answer in a hail sends it.

37
00:01:58,765 --> 00:02:00,538
The objective is the decision,

38
00:02:00,988 --> 00:02:02,014
so it's a page.

39
00:02:02,914 --> 00:02:05,570
The Assay Office at the top, as a ship,

40
00:02:06,020 --> 00:02:09,652
and a line that says their answer will be written down.

41
00:02:11,452 --> 00:02:12,752
And there are two answers.

42
00:02:13,652 --> 00:02:15,899
Pay the fee, and be polite.

43
00:02:16,349 --> 00:02:18,869
Or lean on the clerk, for nothing.

44
00:02:20,669 --> 00:02:22,087
Both of them finish the step.

45
00:02:22,987 --> 00:02:25,930
But they leave the crew in different places with the Deepwell,

46
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:28,885
and that's remembered for the rest of the campaign.

47
00:02:30,685 --> 00:02:31,870
There's one warning, though.

48
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:34,614
The answers are there from the first evening.

49
00:02:35,514 --> 00:02:37,319
A curious crew can ask early,

50
00:02:37,769 --> 00:02:39,956
and it won't count. So word them for that.

51
00:02:41,156 --> 00:02:42,144
Now for the race.

52
00:02:42,594 --> 00:02:44,669
A race is a step with a clock on it.

53
00:02:46,469 --> 00:02:49,283
Before you put one on the spine, here's what I measured.

54
00:02:50,183 --> 00:02:52,478
When the time ran out, the step failed.

55
00:02:52,928 --> 00:02:55,345
And the next step was never revealed.

56
00:02:57,145 --> 00:02:58,712
That's the campaign stopped,

57
00:02:59,162 --> 00:03:01,567
in a save your crew has put ten weeks into.

58
00:03:03,367 --> 00:03:05,564
So a clock never goes on the spine.

59
00:03:06,464 --> 00:03:09,817
And closing the game doesn't undo a clock that ran out.

60
00:03:11,017 --> 00:03:13,081
A clock belongs on a job.

61
00:03:13,981 --> 00:03:16,242
A job that fails costs its penalty,

62
00:03:16,692 --> 00:03:18,236
and the crew can take it again.

63
00:03:20,036 --> 00:03:21,603
This one goes back to the Wren,

64
00:03:22,053 --> 00:03:24,658
so the place from evening one still matters.

65
00:03:26,458 --> 00:03:28,942
And it's tier two, which gives it a date.

66
00:03:29,842 --> 00:03:33,333
The Compact only offers it to a crew at twenty or more.

67
00:03:33,783 --> 00:03:36,204
Your ledger says when that is.

68
00:03:38,004 --> 00:03:40,379
The crew reads its text once they've taken it,

69
00:03:40,829 --> 00:03:42,261
in the Quest Log.

70
00:03:43,161 --> 00:03:45,114
So the clock goes in the text too,

71
00:03:45,564 --> 00:03:47,343
on a line with a little hourglass.

72
00:03:48,543 --> 00:03:51,366
What about a boarding evening, or a ruin, right now?

73
00:03:53,166 --> 00:03:55,279
You have those, as missions of their own.

74
00:03:56,179 --> 00:03:58,250
You can play one as an interlude,

75
00:03:58,700 --> 00:04:00,910
the same people in the same story.

76
00:04:02,710 --> 00:04:04,274
But be exact about it,

77
00:04:04,724 --> 00:04:06,705
because it's another save.

78
00:04:07,605 --> 00:04:09,908
Nothing they do there reaches your universe.

79
00:04:11,708 --> 00:04:14,149
The only bridge is the next evening's hook,

80
00:04:14,599 --> 00:04:16,445
so write that hook after you've played.

81
00:04:17,645 --> 00:04:19,520
Lint says it's clean.

82
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:22,503
There's no lamp run on the list yet.

83
00:04:24,303 --> 00:04:25,817
I pay the levy once,

84
00:04:26,267 --> 00:04:27,155
and there it is.

85
00:04:28,055 --> 00:04:29,984
I take it and engage,

86
00:04:30,434 --> 00:04:32,246
and it's done and paid.

87
00:04:34,046 --> 00:04:35,357
Then it's evening three.

88
00:04:36,257 --> 00:04:38,412
At the Assay Office I hail,

89
00:04:38,862 --> 00:04:40,580
and I choose how to ask.

90
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:43,690
The step shows done,

91
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:46,130
and the way home appears.

92
00:04:47,330 --> 00:04:49,353
So every evening has an owner,

93
00:04:49,803 --> 00:04:52,018
and no style comes twice running.

94
00:04:53,818 --> 00:04:55,257
No clock on the spine.

95
00:04:56,157 --> 00:04:59,415
And an interlude is joined by your writing, and nothing else.

96
00:05:01,215 --> 00:05:04,376
Next time, real people sit down at the table.
